meta {
  name: 02 Exam module needs Pro
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/modelltests
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{studentAccessToken}}
}

docs {
  The line between Start and Pro, and the thing that makes Pro worth buying.

  `/api/modelltests` is the full timed simulation across Hören, Lesen,
  Schreiben and Sprechen, with results afterwards. Pro and Premium only.

  ## What each student gets

  | student | result |
  |---|---|
  | PRO | 200 |
  | PREMIUM | 200 |
  | START | 403 TIER_TOO_LOW |
  | a center student with no tier | 403 TIER_TOO_LOW |
  | an independent student (no center) | **200** |

  Change the student's tier per `00 How to set a student tier` and re-run each
  time.

  ## The refusal

      {
        "error": "TIER_TOO_LOW",
        "tier": "START",
        "requiredTier": "PRO"
      }

  Both numbers travel with it so a client can offer the upgrade the school has
  to buy, rather than showing a bare failure. `tier` comes back as `null`
  rather than a substituted value when the student holds none — never show a
  tier a student does not have.

  It is a 403, not a 401. The student is perfectly authenticated; telling them
  to log in again would send them round a loop that cannot help.

  ## The independent student, and why 200

  An independent student has the exam module TODAY. Gating on tier alone would
  have taken it away from real users for a reason that has nothing to do with
  them, so a student no center governs is admitted. When independent paid
  plans arrive, this is the line that changes.

  ## The stale tier — worth proving by hand

  `students.center_id` is ON DELETE SET NULL while `students.tier` is not, so
  deleting a center leaves the tier behind on the row. Set a student to
  PREMIUM, then release them:

      UPDATE students SET tier = 'PREMIUM' WHERE id = '<student>';
      UPDATE students SET center_id = NULL WHERE id = '<student>';

  They are admitted — but as an ungoverned student, not as a Premium one.
  Confirm with `03 AI quota`: their allowance is 2, not 20. Reading the tier
  without a center would have handed them Premium for ever on the strength of
  a row nobody governs.

  ## Order of refusals

  A student whose center has stopped paying must hear
  403 SUBSCRIPTION_INACTIVE, not TIER_TOO_LOW. Block the center per
  `Subscription Access / 00 How to block and restore` and re-run with a START
  student: the subscription refusal wins, because "your school stopped paying"
  and "your school needs to buy Pro" are different problems and only one of
  them is theirs to fix.
}
